## Introduction
An *n-gram model* analyzes training data to determine how frequently specific word sequences occur. For example, given the phrase “my house is,” the model examines all words that follow “my” in the dataset. If “house” is the most common continuation, the model predicts “house.”

In this Post, I will cover the mathematical basics of modern LMs and train my own N-Gram Model based on my lecture notes. I'll take a mathematical dive into different token selection functions and prove why the maximum likelihood assumption leads to periodic repetition.


## Mathematical Basics
> This section is largely based on the book Speech and Language Processing by Jurafsky & Martin  [-@NLP2026, ch. I.3]

Most language models use context consisting of $n-1$ tokens $C:= w_1w_2\cdots w_{n-1}$ to predict the *nth* token $w_n$ and describe the probability for every
next token with $P(w_n|w_{1:n-1}) = P(w_n|C)$. N-Gram models limit this context
by assuming $\approx P(w_n| w_{n-N+1} \cdots w_{n-1})$. In the extreme case of a *bigram model*
($N=2$) this assumption simplifies to $P(w_n|C)\approx P(w_n|w_{n-1})$, we call
this the *markov assumption*. The probability of outputting a sentence $s=w_{1}w_{2}\dots w_{m}$ with token-length $m$ based on context token $c$ is $P(s)=\prod_{w\in s}P(w)$. I define $S_{N}$ as the set of all sentences with length N. And $s_{w_n}$
We define $T(a | B)$ as the number of occurrences of $a$ with the sentence $B$ preceeding. The next token selection is decided by $Sel: C \longrightarrow w_n$.

In our case, we define $P(w_{n}|C)=\frac{ T(w_{n}| w_{n-N+1 \cdots w_{n-1}})}{ T(w_{n})}$, simplifying to $P(w_{n}|C) = \frac{T(w_n|w_{n-1})}{T(w_n)}$ with bigrams.

<!-- Since we know that $\sum_wP(w|C)=1$, we can choose tokens easily by selecting based on $P$.  -->
### Greedy decoding always ends in a loop
Let's take the *maximum likelihood assumption* of picking with $Sel(C) := \max_{w_{i}} (P(w_{i}|C))$. 

Before we improve $Sel$, let's prove *why* choosing greedily will fail.
#### Proof
> Adapted from @knuth1997

We define a greedy $Sel$ as:

$$f: V \longrightarrow V, \quad f(w) := \operatorname*{arg\,max}_{w' \in V} P(w' \mid w),$$ Token selection function under the maximum likelihood assumption

Given vocabulary $V$ with $|V| = v < \infty$:
Generation from a seed $w_{0}$ is then nothing but iterating:
$$w_{t} = f(w_{t-1}) = f^{t}(w_{0}).$$

> **Theorem.** For every $w_{0} \in V$ the greedy sequence $(w_{t})_{t \ge 0}$ is
> *eventually periodic*: there are $i,j \in \mathbb{R}$ where $i < j \le v$ such that $w_{i} = w_{j}$, and the sequence repeats with an arbitrary period $p$: $f^{p+1}(w_{i})=w_j$

**Proof.** Look at the first $v+1$ tokens $w_{0}, w_{1}, \dots, w_{v}$. They are $v+1$
elements drawn from a set of only $v$ tokens, so by the pigeonhole principle two of them must
coincide: $w_{i} = w_{j}$ for some $0 \le i < j \le v$. Because $f$ is a *function*:

$$\forall k \ge 0:\quad w_{i+k} = f^{k}(w_{i}) = f^{k}(w_{j}) = w_{j+k}$$

So the sequence is periodic with period $p = j - i$ from index $i$ on. $\blacksquare$

#### What do we learn from that?
1. **Repetition is structural, not a bug of the dataset.** Any finitely large dataset doesn't fix the underlying problem.
2. **The model can produce at most $|V|$ distinct texts** 
3. **Termination is luck.** We can't guarantee the loop contains a EOS.

### Markov
To prevent this, we sample from $P(\cdot|C)$. Thus, $f$ is no longer a *function*, but a *stochastic kernel* of a markov chain. This leads to the key insight that a *bigram model* with this $Sel$ is equivalent to a markov chain, where nodes are tokens and edge weights are $P(w_n|w_{n-1})$. 

We can also model higher-order N-Grams as markov chains, where the number of nodes increases exponentially with $V^N$.
### The better selection Funct
> This section is based on @NLP2026 too

The maximum likelihood assumption fails in the real world. No matter how big the training set, some sentences will never be able to be generated. That leaves the model locked on a subset $\subset$ of real language.

One option is Laplace Smoothing:

$$P_\text{Laplace}(w_{i})=\frac{ T(w_{i} + 1}{ T(w_{n})}$$ **Laplace smoothing**: Just adding 1 for every token

This leads to this Prediction function in bigram models:

$$P_\text{Laplace} (w_{n}|w_{n-1})=\frac{T(w_{n-1}w_n)+1}{\sum\limits_{w}(T(w_{n-1}w)+1)}\overset{\sum1=V}{=}\frac{T(w_{n-1}w_{n})+1}{T(w_{n-1})+V}$$
@NLP2026, eq. 3.26

#### Laplace charges a proportional tax on evidence
*What count would
have produced this probability without smoothing?* Solving
$P_\text{Laplace}=\frac{T^{*}}{T(w_{n-1})}$ for $T^{*}$ gives the **effective count**

$$T^{*}(w_{n-1}w_{n}) = \bigl(T(w_{n-1}w_{n})+1\bigr)\cdot\underbrace{\frac{T(w_{n-1})}{T(w_{n-1})+V}}_{=:d(w_{n-1})}$$
The discount factor $d$ does not depend on the bigram at all, only on the context. So Laplace is a
*proportional* tax: every continuation of $w_{n-1}$ is scaled by the same $d$, no matter whether we
saw it 500 times or never.

That factor is brutal at the vocabulary sizes we are dealing with. My vault has $V\approx 2\cdot10^{4}$ types, and a decently frequent context like `Gradient` might have $T(w_{n-1})=200$. Then
$d \approx \frac{200}{20200}\approx 0.0099$, and the bigram `Gradient Descent`, observed 50 times, ends up with an effective count of roughly $0.5$. We spent a whole corpus collecting that evidence and then threw away $99\%$ of it to fund $V$ continuations that never happened.

#### Lets add more!
Add-$k$ smoothing, $P_{\text{add-}k}(w_{n}|w_{n-1})=\frac{T(w_{n-1}w_{n})+k}{T(w_{n-1})+kV}$, only
changes the tax *rate*. The structural problem survives: the amount of mass taken from observed
events scales with $V$ and with nothing about the context except its total count. A single $k$ tuned
on held-out data is always a compromise between contexts we know a lot about and contexts we saw
three times.

#### Discount, don't rescale
Both scale *multiplicatively*. But held-out counts show the MLE error is **additive**: bigrams seen $r$ times recur about $r-0.75$ times [-@NLP2026]. So subtract a fixed $\delta$ instead. Normalization then forces the freed mass to be

$$\lambda_{w_{n-1}}=\frac{\delta\,N_{1+}(w_{n-1}\bullet)}{T(w_{n-1})},\qquad N_{1+}(w_{n-1}\bullet)=\bigl|\{w: T(w_{n-1}w)>0\}\bigr|$$

so diverse contexts leak more. It is only consulted in *unseen* contexts, so count how many distinct contexts $w$ appears in, not how often. `Descent` is frequent, yet almost always follows `Gradient`.
### Knesner-Ney
$$P_{\text{KneserNey}}(w_{i}|w_{i-1})=\underbrace{\frac{\max (T(w_{i-1}w_{i})-\delta,0)}{\sum_{w}T(w_{i-1}w)}}_{\text{measured evidence, deficient by}\ 1-\lambda_{w_{i-1}}} +\lambda_{w_{i-1}}P_{KN}(w_{i})$$ Bigram KneserNey-Smoothing. Adapted from @NEY1994

We arrive at Knesner-Ney. 
The **right term** spends exactly the deficit the **left one creates**. $\lambda$ is normalized, so $P_{KN}$ is freely chosen.

### Perplexity
For LM evaluation, we use **Perplexity**. It is the exponentiated average negative log-likelihood the model assigns to held-out text:

$$\text{PP}(w_{1:M}) = \exp\left(-\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^{M}\log P(w_{i}\mid w_{i-N+1:i-1})\right)$$ **Perplexity**: the model's average surprise per token

Read it as an effective branching factor: *how many words is the model effectively choosing between at each step?* A uniform model over my vocabulary has a perplexity of exactly 15,622, because it is choosing uniformly between 15,622 options. Lower is better [@NLP2026].

## Building an N-Gram Model
I was able to extract 585 .md files, skipping the following:

```python
forbidden = [
    r"*/.claude/*",
    r"*/.obsidian/*",
    r"*.excalidraw.md",
    r"*/node_modules/*",
]
```

**Preprocessing:** Markdown is not prose, since markup is far more repetitive than text, so a model trained on the raw vault would spend most of its probability mass learning that `[` follows `]`. Twenty-one ordered regex substitutions normalise this, anything carrying display text is reduced to it (aliased wikilinks, markdown links, callout headers), anything else is deleted rather than replaced (frontmatter, code, math, embeds, URLs, HTML, hashtags, rules). Pure markers (headings, bullets, blockquotes, checkboxes, emphasis) are stripped. Because a lecture note is a stack of grammatically unrelated bullet fragments, each **line** becomes its own training sequence. All exact duplicate notes are dropped by content hash, since the same content being in training and validation set would defeat the point. 

