> 277k tokens is *very small*. For scale, GPT-3 was trained on roughly 300 billion. 

### How much context is worth paying for?
So I trained one model per order, from $N=0$ (uniform) up to $N=19$, and measured each on the same held-out documents:

<div class="table-scroll">
	<table>
		<tbody>
			<tr><th scope="row">N</th><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>6</td><td>8</td><td>10</td><td>12</td><td>15</td><td>19</td></tr>
			<tr><th scope="row">Perplexity</th><td>15622</td><td>801</td><td>137.8</td><td>42.6</td><td>28.8</td><td>25.5</td><td>24.0</td><td>22.8</td><td>22.5</td><td>22.5</td><td>23.0</td><td>23.9</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
</div>

Improvement seems to be lorarithmic. The "context window of 1" divides perplexity by almost six, and the trigram cuts it by another two thirds. After $N=4$ perplexity doesn't seem to improve.

Later **it surprisingly goes back up**. Past $N=12$, more context makes the model measurably worse. This seemingly is because it has fewer training data for longer lines. At $N=3$, 15% of held-out tokens arrive with a context the model has never seen; by $N=12$ it is 35%.
### What it actually writes
Perplexity doesn't paint the whole Picture. So a test run: same seed, same prompt, one model per order:

```text
--- N = 1 ---
the main
 of
: ,
:
.
 . 1
 the 3 the , b
die

--- N = 2 ---
the main process
:
for all
x lecture 03 .
removed and the
in order to a basis of b ,
fixed a 4 , then there is true .

--- N = 3 ---
the main process before processes are launched when using a kernel , the
removed property from the other files : , email 42 , concat name , :
the trainer no longer randomly selects a seed no longer returns a triplet 18390
: there is a subset of examples for pytorch 2.4 .
if is even .

--- N = 5 ---
the main academic risks are : unclosed gaps in securityprivacy weeks 14 15 , the rsa calculation error , and the missing .
you can decompress drawing data with the command palette : decompress current excalidraw file .

--- N = 10 ---
the main academic risks are : unclosed gaps in securityprivacy weeks 14 15 , the rsa calculation error , and the missing buffer-overflow payload practice .
a graph is planar if it can be drawn on a paper such that no edges cross each other
```

Every line break is the model emitting `</s>` and starting a fresh sequence, so the number of lines is itself a signal: the higher the order, the longer it keeps going before it decides EOS.

$N=1$ is function words and punctuation — the model has no context, so it produces the most common tokens in the language, which are the ones carrying no meaning. $N=3$ is the interesting one: locally grammatical, globally nonsense. *"the main process before processes are launched when using a kernel"* actually quite good, you'd have to secondguess to figure out it is nonsense.

And then $N=5$ and $N=10$ stop writing and start **quoting**.  *"A graph is planar if it can be drawn on a paper such that no edges cross each other"* is a line from my notes on discrete modelling, reproduced word for word.

### The minimum is a lie
That is just an impression though, so let's measure it. For each order I sampled a few hundred lines, cut them into 8-grams, and checked how many of those 8-grams appear word for word in the training split and, separately, how many of the generated lines are entire lines of the vault reproduced exactly:

<div class="fig-row">
	<figure class="plain">
		<img src="/img/markov/Citations.png" alt="A chart visualising Citations for different N" />
	</figure>
	<figure class="plain">
		<img src="/img/markov/Perplexity.png" alt="A chart visualising Perplexity for different N" />
	</figure>
</div>

The rows move together. Every point of perplexity I bought past $N=3$ was paid for in originality: at the perplexity minimum, **two out of three 8-word spans the model produces are copied straight out of my notes, and half of the lines it writes are completely out of my notes.**

At $N=10$, 62% of the contexts the model knows occur exactly *once* in the entire corpus. The model isn't choosing. It is looking up the rest of the line.

Which brings us back to its own proof. Greedy decoding fails because it is a function on a finite set, so it must eventually cycle. Sampling fixes that, but at high $N$ it stops repeating *itself* and starts repeating *me*. Both are a model with nothing of its own to say, and only the first one is visible in the loss.

If you want the most original model, I suggest $N=3$ or $4$: still recombining, just before the counts get sparse enough to overfit. **→ The best-scoring model and the best model are not the same.**

### One model, two languages
My notes are bilingual, and both go into one model with one vocabulary. So it code-switches mid-line, as I do in my notes:

```text
fixed an issue to avoid validation loop : : set aller möglichen hypothesen nach der hypothesis
der lokale daemon muss als eigenständige for any
```

## Improvement opportunities
- **Estimate the discount instead of guessing it.** $\delta$ is hard-coded to 0.75 throughout. Let's change that and use a separate delta for N=1,2,3+: $\delta = \frac{n_1}{n_1 + 2n_2}$ [-@NLP2026].
- **Top-k sampling**. We could store the k most next $w_i$ and $P(w_i| C)$, normalized such that $\sum\limits_{i=1}^{k}P(w_{i}|C)=1$ in a key-value store and run the Markov kernel only on those fixed number of tokens. This would mean Inference goes from $\Theta(N\cdot V)$ per token to $\Theta(1)$, independent of vocabulary and order. Note that this isn't a lossless operation [@fan2018].

## Limitation
There is a ceiling, and it is not an engineering problem. An n-gram model stores a table indexed by context, so its parameter count grows as $V^{N}$. With my modest 15,622-token vocabulary, a 10-gram table has $15622^{10} \approx 10^{42}$ cells. I observed 277,208 of them. Every additional token of context multiplies the space by 15,622 and adds zero new evidence.

That is the wall the whole field hit, and the reason the next idea had to be structurally different rather than merely bigger. This ceiling was already visible in the smoothing literature by 2001 [@goodman2001], and still standing in 2007 when Google trained 5-grams on two trillion tokens of web text and watched the gains go logarithmic.

## Conclusion
*So: what does a context window of 1 get you?* $\rightarrow$ A perplexity of 137.8, sentences that are grammatical for about four words at a time, and a model small enough an Arduino would be able to run it easily.

It doesn't get you anywhere near the level of competence modern LLMs have and finds no relation between *similar* tokens. You can't *embed* tokens or find any semantically valuable information in the models.
